{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI in Education","title":"AI Use Disclosure","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting ai use disclosure to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"07","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/03/06"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_calibration","cat531-supplement_03_07_lo_01_direct_check"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-01":["cat531-supplement_03_07_lo_01_direct_check"],"cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-03":["cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-04":["cat531-supplement_03_07_lo_01_direct_check"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# AI Use Disclosure\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting AI Use Disclosure, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_ai_in_education\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/03-07-ai-use-disclosure.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for AI Use Disclosure: AI Use Disclosure, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nSchools have rushed to require that students disclose their AI use, while teachers quietly use AI to write feedback, report-card comments, and recommendation letters without disclosing anything. That asymmetry is not a small inconsistency; it is a credibility problem that students notice instantly. A teacher who demands transparency she does not practice teaches students that disclosure is a rule for the powerless, not an ethical norm. Getting disclosure right, for yourself first, is therefore foundational to having any standing to ask it of students.\n\nThis section matters because disclosure is widely misunderstood as either a confession or a liability shield, when it is neither. Good disclosure does two specific jobs: it gives the reader the information they need to judge what they are reading, and it keeps accountability where it belongs. The most common failures are a one-word label that tells the reader nothing (\"AI-assisted\"), and the dangerous assumption that disclosing AI use transfers responsibility for accuracy to the tool. A teacher who understands disclosure can be honest about AI in a high-trust document without diluting the trust, because the disclosure informs the reader and the teacher still owns every word.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher uses a chatbot to draft report-card comments from her gradebook notes, edits them, and sends them home. A parent later asks her in person about a specific phrase praising her son's \"growing leadership,\" and the teacher cannot recall the incident it refers to, because the AI invented a plausible-sounding detail she did not catch. This failure mode is well documented: as of 2026, a current assistant such as ChatGPT, Claude, or Microsoft Copilot reliably produces fluent, authoritative-sounding but fabricated specifics, often using more confident language when hallucinating than when stating facts (MIT Sloan Teaching & Learning Technologies, 2025). The parent's trust takes a hit, and not only because the detail was wrong: she realizes the warm, personal-sounding comment was partly machine-written, which she was never told.\n\nNow reverse it. A different teacher discloses at the top of the comments: \"I drafted these from my own gradebook notes with AI assistance, then edited and verified each comment against my records.\" A parent reading that knows exactly what they are looking at and can calibrate accordingly, and when every specific claim turns out to be accurate, the disclosure increases trust rather than eroding it. Same tool, opposite outcomes. The difference is whether the disclosure was present, specific, and backed by the teacher actually owning the accuracy.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEffective AI disclosure rests on four properties, and most bad disclosures fail one of them.\n\n- **Reciprocity.** Transparency cannot be a one-way demand. If you require students to disclose AI use, professional integrity requires you to disclose your own in comparable situations. The standard you set for the less powerful party you must meet yourself; otherwise the rule is about authority, not ethics.\n- **Specificity.** \"AI-assisted\" is nearly content-free, because it does not tell the reader what AI did versus what you did. Useful disclosure names the **division of labor**: what the tool generated, what you contributed, and what you verified. The reader needs to know whether AI drafted everything and you skimmed it, or you wrote it and AI checked grammar; those are very different things they cannot distinguish from a single label.\n- **Accountability stays with the human.** This is the property people get backward. Disclosure informs the reader; it does not transfer responsibility. Saying \"this was AI-assisted\" does not make the tool answerable for an error; you are still fully accountable for every claim you distribute under your name. Disclosure is an act of honesty, not a disclaimer that offloads blame.\n- **Psychological safety.** Disclosure norms only produce honesty if honesty is safe. If admitting AI use is automatically punished, people hide it, and you get less transparency, not more. A workable norm distinguishes permitted, disclosed use from prohibited use, so that disclosing allowed assistance never feels like a confession.\n\nThe organizing idea: **disclosure is for the reader, accountability is for the writer.** A disclosure that is vague fails the reader; a disclosure that is treated as a liability shield fails the writer's responsibility. Good disclosure does both jobs at once, telling the reader precisely what they are reading while leaving every error squarely on the human who sent it.\n\n**Where the law sits.** These four properties are ethical, but the accountability property also has a statutory anchor. AI-assisted analysis or comments about an identifiable student become part of that student's FERPA-protected educational record, and FERPA keeps accountability with the institution and the teacher acting for it even when a vendor or general-purpose tool generated the text. Disclosure norms are also hardening: across 2024-2026 most U.S. states have issued K-12 AI guidance, and some, including Tennessee (since March 2024) and Ohio, now mandate district-level AI-use policies, with several states emphasizing transparency over punitive detection.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher labels an AI-assisted recommendation letter This was created with AI assistance. Which disclosure property does that label fail most clearly?\" explanation=\"The label is content-free: it does not name the division of labor (what the AI did versus what the teacher contributed and verified), so it fails specificity. It also reads as a hedge that tries to shift blame, but the most clear-cut failure is that the reader still cannot tell what they are reading.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Reciprocity, because the teacher did not require it of students\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Specificity, because it does not name what the AI did versus what the teacher did\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Psychological safety, because it punishes honesty\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It fails no property; the label is sufficient\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare two disclosures on the same AI-assisted college recommendation letter, a high-stakes document.\n\nWeak: \"This letter was created with AI assistance.\" A reader cannot tell whether the teacher knows this student at all or whether a chatbot invented the praise. Worse, the phrasing reads as a hedge, as if to say \"if anything is off, blame the tool.\" It fails specificity and quietly attempts to shed accountability.\n\nStrong: \"I wrote this letter from my own observations of [student] over two years; I used AI to tighten the prose, and I verified that every accomplishment and quality described reflects work I personally witnessed.\" This tells the reader exactly what the AI did (polished wording) and what it did not (supply the substance), so they can trust the content while knowing the language was assisted. It is reciprocal with what the teacher asks of students, it is specific about the division of labor, and it explicitly keeps accountability with the teacher (\"I verified... I witnessed\"). The strong version uses the same tool but produces trust instead of suspicion, because it serves the reader and owns the result. The evidence here is mixed, so the claim must stay precise: Schilke and Reimann (2025), across thirteen experiments, found that disclosing AI use can actually lower trust by reducing perceived legitimacy. The defensible position is therefore narrower: specific, verified disclosure can preserve or build trust where a vague label or hidden use would erode it, but disclosure alone does not guarantee higher trust. If the teacher had instead let AI invent achievements, no disclosure could rescue it, because the problem would no longer be transparency but fabrication.\n\n## Worked Decision: Writing an Honest Disclosure Statement\n\nFollow a teacher's annotated before/after judgment while writing a disclosure note for AI-assisted report-card comments families will read.\n\n1. **Before (no disclosure, one-sided rule):** \"I will use the chatbot to draft all comments and send them as my own. Students must disclose their AI use, but mine is private.\" The reciprocity of transparency is missing.\n2. **Reciprocity named:** Annotate the asymmetry. If AI substantially shaped what families read about their child, honesty requires the teacher to disclose her use, just as she requires of students.\n3. **Specificity edit:** Annotate the vague label. A first draft note reads only \"Some comments were AI-assisted.\" She rewrites it to name the division of labor: \"I used AI to draft a first version from my gradebook notes, then I edited every comment for accuracy and added each child's specific examples myself.\"\n4. **After (accountability kept):** \"I disclose my AI use and I verified every fact against my own records. If a comment is wrong, that is on me, not the tool. I rejected three AI-generated comments that did not match what I observed.\" Disclosure informs the reader; it does not move the responsibility.\n5. **Evidence trail:** The decision is justified by reciprocal transparency and by **teacher professional judgment** (verify, then own the result). The rejected comments and the discarded vague label are recorded with reasons.\n\nStep 1 to step 4 captures the section's lesson: disclosure is reciprocal, specific about who did what, framed so honesty is safe, and never a transfer of accountability for accuracy.\n\nNow write the call yourself. The branching scenario below puts you at the moment of sending AI-assisted report-card comments; choose how you disclose and where accountability lands.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Disclosing AI-assisted report-card comments\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"You drafted report-card comments with a chatbot from your gradebook notes. You require students to disclose their AI use. How do you handle your own?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Send them as my own; my use is private\",\"next\":\"hidden\",\"feedback\":\"That is the double standard that destroys the norm credibility.\"},{\"label\":\"Disclose, because reciprocity means I meet the standard I set for students\",\"next\":\"reciprocal\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Transparency cannot be a one-way demand.\"},{\"label\":\"Add the label some comments were AI-assisted and send\",\"next\":\"vague\",\"feedback\":\"Technically disclosed, but it tells the reader nothing about the division of labor.\"}]},\"hidden\":{\"prompt\":\"A parent later learns the warm comment was machine-written and was never told. Trust drops. Reconsider?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Disclose my use the way I require of students\",\"next\":\"reciprocal\",\"feedback\":\"You restored reciprocity.\"}]},\"vague\":{\"prompt\":\"A parent cannot tell whether a machine judged her child or you did. How do you fix the wording?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Name the division of labor: what AI drafted, what I wrote and verified\",\"next\":\"reciprocal\",\"feedback\":\"Specificity restored; the reader can now calibrate.\"}]},\"reciprocal\":{\"prompt\":\"You write: I drafted a first version from my gradebook notes with AI, then I edited every comment for accuracy and added each child specific examples myself. One AI comment claims growing leadership you cannot place. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Reject the unverifiable comment; accountability stays with me, not the tool\",\"next\":\"strong\",\"feedback\":\"Disclosure informs the reader; it never transfers blame.\"},{\"label\":\"Keep it; the disclosure covers any error\",\"next\":\"shield\",\"feedback\":\"Disclosure is not a liability shield; the error is still yours.\"}]},\"shield\":{\"prompt\":\"Non-defensible outcome: you treated disclosure as a shield and distributed an unverified, possibly fabricated claim under your name.\",\"choices\":[]},\"strong\":{\"prompt\":\"Defensible outcome: reciprocal, specific about who did what, honest about permitted use, and you kept accountability by rejecting the comment you could not verify.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore sending AI-assisted work, test the disclosure against its four properties:\n\n1. **Reciprocity.** Are you disclosing what you would require a student to disclose in the same situation?\n2. **Specificity.** Does the disclosure name the division of labor (what AI did, what you did, what you verified), not just slap on an \"AI-assisted\" label?\n3. **Accountability.** Is it clear that you, not the tool, are responsible for every claim? Disclosure informs; it never transfers blame.\n4. **Safety.** Does your classroom norm make disclosing permitted use safe, so honesty is rewarded rather than punished?\n\nDisclose when AI materially shaped what the reader receives, be specific about who did what, and own the result regardless of what the tool produced.\n\nMatch each disclosure property to the failure it prevents. Holding the four apart is what lets you diagnose why a given disclosure is weak.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Reciprocity\",\"definition\":\"Prevents the double standard of requiring student disclosure while hiding your own\"},{\"term\":\"Specificity\",\"definition\":\"Prevents the content-free label by naming what AI did versus what you did and verified\"},{\"term\":\"Accountability\",\"definition\":\"Prevents disclosure from being misused as a liability shield for an error\"},{\"term\":\"Psychological safety\",\"definition\":\"Prevents a punitive norm that drives AI use underground and yields less honesty\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most damaging misreading treats disclosure as a liability shield, as if \"AI-assisted\" excuses an error; it does not, because accountability for distributed work is always the human's. A second is the vague label, which technically discloses while telling the reader nothing useful about the division of labor. A third is the double standard: requiring student disclosure while hiding your own, which destroys the norm's credibility. A fourth is building a punitive norm where any admitted AI use is penalized, which simply drives use underground and yields less honesty. Finally, some treat disclosure as the whole ethical question, when disclosure cannot fix a deeper problem like fabricated content; transparency about a fabrication is still a fabrication.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the four properties to a realistic case. A teacher uses AI to help write individualized feedback on twenty-eight lab reports and must decide whether and how to disclose to students.\n\nReciprocity first: her syllabus requires students to note AI assistance on their work, so consistency requires her to disclose hers; skipping it would teach the double standard directly. Specificity next: a label like \"feedback was AI-assisted\" would leave students unsure whether a machine judged their work or a teacher did. She writes instead, \"I drafted phrasing for some feedback with AI, but every score and every specific comment about your data is my own judgment based on reading your report.\" That names the division of labor precisely.\n\nAccountability: she does not let the disclosure soften her responsibility. If a comment misreads a student's data, that is her error to correct, not the tool's, and she frames it that way. Safety: she pairs the disclosure with a clear message that her use was a permitted, deliberate choice, modeling that disclosed, allowed AI use is normal and not a confession, which makes students more willing to be honest about their own. The disclosure ends up strengthening the integrity culture she is trying to build, precisely because it is reciprocal, specific, accountable, and safe.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this topic separates the two jobs a disclosure does, informing the reader and locating accountability, and checks that a given disclosure does both. As you read, keep a two-column note with the **disclosure as written** on the left and the **property it satisfies or violates** on the right (reciprocity, specificity, accountability, safety). A disclosure that satisfies all four is rare on a first draft.\n\nA strong analysis can answer four questions about any disclosure:\n\n1. Would I require a student to disclose this same use, and am I holding myself to that?\n2. Does the wording tell the reader what AI did versus what I did?\n3. Is it unmistakable that I remain accountable for accuracy?\n4. Does my classroom norm make honest disclosure of permitted use safe?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour tension map is strong when it resolves the real conflict in this topic (honesty versus the fear that disclosure undermines trust or invites blame) by writing a disclosure that is reciprocal, specific, and accountability-keeping. Show a weak label, the specific version you would use instead, and your reasoning about who stays responsible. The weakest maps say \"be transparent about AI\"; the strongest show an actual disclosure that names the division of labor and explicitly keeps the error on the human.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact would read: \"I disclosed my AI use in the same way I require of students, named exactly what the tool drafted versus what I wrote and verified, and made clear that I remain accountable for every claim; I rejected the vague 'AI-assisted' label because it would inform the reader of nothing and read like an attempt to shift blame.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove disclosure from a teacher's own work to designing the disclosure expectation you set for students. The four properties invert into design requirements: the norm you write must be reciprocal (you model it), specific (students know what level of detail to give), accountability-preserving (disclosing AI use does not excuse a student from owning their submitted work), and safe (disclosed, permitted use is not punished). Building a student disclosure policy that embodies the same four properties you apply to yourself, rather than a one-line \"you must report AI use,\" is the test that you understand disclosure as an ethical structure rather than a label.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_in_education\",\"ai_use_disclosure\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/03-07-ai-use-disclosure-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"03.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 03.07. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_ai_use_disclosure\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). Technological pedagogical content knowledge: A framework for teacher knowledge. Teachers College Record, 108(6), 1017-1054. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Schilke, O., & Reimann, M. (2025). The transparency dilemma: How AI disclosure erodes trust. Organizational Behavior and Human Decision Processes, 181, 104405. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.obhdp.2025.104405\n- Chan, C. K. Y. (2023). A comprehensive AI policy education framework for university teaching and learning. International Journal of Educational Technology in Higher Education, 20, 38. https://doi.org/10.1186/s41239-023-00408-3\n- Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA), 20 U.S.C. 1232g; 34 CFR Part 99. U.S. Department of Education. https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/faq/what-ferpa\n- Agile Education Marketing. (2025). From suggestion to requirement: K-12 school district AI policy in 2025. https://agile-ed.com/resources/k12-school-district-ai-policy-2025/\n- MIT Sloan Teaching & Learning Technologies. (2025). When AI gets it wrong: Addressing AI hallucinations and bias. https://mitsloanedtech.mit.edu/ai/basics/addressing-ai-hallucinations-and-bias/\n- Selwyn, N. (2014). Distrusting Educational Technology: Critical Questions for Changing Times. Routledge. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2019). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. Learning, Media and Technology, 45(3), 251-269. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_use_disclosure","stem":"A teacher uses a chatbot to draft personalized comments on report cards and sends them without saying so, while requiring students to disclose any AI help. What principle is being violated?","options":["None; disclosure rules apply only to students.","Transparency is reciprocal; if AI substantially shaped the comments families read, the teacher should disclose her use too.","Teachers should never use AI at all.","Students should also stop disclosing."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disclosure norms apply to teachers as well as students. When AI substantially shapes communication to families, the teacher's use should be disclosed.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_disclosure_only_for_students","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_use_disclosure","stem":"A student turns in a project tagged only \"Made with AI.\" Why is this disclosure inadequate, and what would make it meaningful?","options":["It is fine; the label says everything a reader needs.","It does not say what AI did versus what the student did; meaningful disclosure names the AI's role, the student's role, and what was verified or rejected.","The label should be larger and bolder.","The student should remove the label to avoid suspicion."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A blanket label does not let a reader judge the work. Meaningful disclosure specifies the division of labor and the verification done.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_disclosure_label_is_enough","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-02","cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_use_disclosure","stem":"An assignment permits AI for outlining. A capable student hides that she used AI to outline because she fears disclosure will look like cheating. What should the teacher's disclosure norm make clear?","options":["That any disclosed AI use will lower the grade.","That disclosing permitted AI use is expected honesty and is not penalized, so transparency feels safe rather than incriminating.","That students should never mention AI.","That only struggling students need to disclose."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"If disclosure feels like confessing guilt, students hide allowed use. The norm must frame transparent, permitted use as expected and unpenalized.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_disclosure_equals_guilt","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_ai_use_disclosure_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_use_disclosure","stem":"A teacher submits an AI-assisted grant narrative that contains a fabricated statistic, then says, \"I disclosed that I used AI, so the error is on the tool.\" What is the most accurate judgment?","options":["Correct; disclosure moves responsibility to the AI.","Disclosure informs the reader but does not transfer accountability; the teacher who submitted the narrative still owns its accuracy and should have verified the statistic.","The statistic does not matter once AI is disclosed.","Only the grant agency is responsible for checking."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disclosure is about transparency, not absolution. The human submitter remains accountable for verifying accuracy regardless of disclosure.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_disclosure_replaces_responsibility","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531-supplement_03_07_lo_01_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-01","cat531-supplement-03-07-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_disclosure_only_for_students","pattern":"disclosure_seen_as_student_only_duty","description":"Believing disclosure of AI use is a rule only students must follow, not teachers","trigger":"teacher uses AI to write feedback or recommendation letters without disclosing it","feedback":"Transparency runs both ways. If AI substantially shaped feedback, grades, or a recommendation, disclose your use to the affected reader, just as you ask students to disclose theirs.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_disclosure_label_is_enough","pattern":"generic_label_treated_as_full_disclosure","description":"Assuming a blanket label like \"AI was used\" satisfies meaningful disclosure","trigger":"student or teacher tags work \"made with AI\" without saying what AI did","feedback":"Meaningful disclosure states what the AI did, what the human did, and what was verified or rejected. A bare label is not enough for a reader to judge the work.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_disclosure_equals_guilt","pattern":"disclosure_conflated_with_admitting_wrongdoing","description":"Treating disclosure of allowed AI use as a confession that lowers the grade","trigger":"student hides permitted AI assistance because disclosure feels like admitting cheating","feedback":"Disclosing allowed use is honesty, not guilt. Make clear that transparent, permitted use is expected and is not penalized, so students do not hide it.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_7_disclosure_replaces_responsibility","pattern":"disclosure_mistaken_for_accountability_transfer","description":"Believing that disclosing AI use shifts responsibility for errors onto the tool","trigger":"author blames the AI for a mistake because the AI was disclosed","feedback":"Disclosure does not transfer accountability. The human who submits the work still owns its accuracy; disclosure informs the reader, it does not excuse errors.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"fffd03858e75a553b1b066bbb8b0a0989ab6baeac7338747e6b94a2c5e1f9832","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}